// ============================================================
// 🌳 LeetCode 104 — Maximum Depth of Binary Tree
// ============================================================
//
// ✅ Problem:
// Given the root of a binary tree, return *its maximum depth*.
//
// The maximum depth is the number of nodes along the longest path
// from the root node down to the farthest leaf node.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 🔹 Example:
// Input: [3,9,20,null,null,15,7]
//
//        3
//       / \
//      9  20
//         / \
//        15  7
//
// Output: 3
// Explanation: Longest path = 3 → 20 → 15 or 3 → 20 → 7
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 🔹 Intuition:
// To find the maximum depth, we must go to the *bottom-most node*
// of each branch. Hence, we calculate the depth of left and right
// subtrees recursively and take the maximum of both.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 🧠 Recursive Algorithm (DFS Approach):
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Step 1️⃣: If root == NULL → return 0 (no depth).
// Step 2️⃣: Recursively find depth of left subtree → lh = maxDepth(root->left)
// Step 3️⃣: Recursively find depth of right subtree → rh = maxDepth(root->right)
// Step 4️⃣: Add +1 for the current node → return 1 + max(lh, rh)
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 🧩 Dry Run Example:
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Tree: 
//        1
//       / \
//      2   3
//     / \
//    4   5
//
// maxDepth(1)
//   → 1 + max(maxDepth(2), maxDepth(3))
//   → 1 + max(1 + max(maxDepth(4), maxDepth(5)), 1)
//   → 1 + max(1 + max(1,1), 1)
//   → 1 + max(2,1)
//   ✅ Output = 3
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ⚙️ Interview Follow-Ups:
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Q1️⃣: Can you solve this iteratively (using BFS)?
//  → Yes. Use a queue and count levels (level-order traversal).
//
// Q2️⃣: What’s the difference between “height” and “depth”?
//  → Depth: distance from root to node.
//    Height: distance from node to deepest leaf.
//    For root → both are same numerically.
//
// Q3️⃣: Why recursion works well here?
//  → Because tree problems are naturally recursive: each subtree is
//    a smaller version of the same problem.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ⚠️ Edge Cases:
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Empty Tree (root = NULL) → Output: 0
// 2️⃣ Single Node → Output: 1
// 3️⃣ Left-skewed tree (only left children) → Depth = #nodes
// 4️⃣ Right-skewed tree (only right children) → Depth = #nodes
//
// ✅ All these are automatically handled by recursion.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// ⏱️ Time Complexity: O(N)
//    - Every node is visited once.
//
// 💾 Space Complexity: O(H)
//    - H = height of tree (recursion stack).
//    - Worst case: O(N) for skewed tree.
//    - Best case: O(log N) for balanced tree.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

class Solution {
public:
    int maxDepth(TreeNode* root) {
        // Base Case: If tree is empty, depth = 0
        if (root == NULL) 
            return 0;

        // Recursive calls to find left and right subtree depth
        int lh = maxDepth(root->left);
        int rh = maxDepth(root->right);

        // Return max depth among left & right + 1 for current node
        return 1 + max(lh, rh);
    }
};

